faq:
  - q: "What is the difference between a career coach and a career counselor?"
    a: "Career counselors typically hold a master's degree in counseling or a related field and are trained in psychological assessment, mental health awareness, and vocational testing. They are often found in academic institutions (university career centers) or government programs. Career coaches are typically hired professionals who draw on industry experience, functional expertise, and coaching methodology to help clients with job search strategy, positioning, interview preparation, and salary negotiation. The key difference: counselors are trained in assessment and exploration; coaches are trained in execution and outcomes."
  - q: "Who should see a career counselor vs. a career coach?"
    a: "Career counselor: if you are early in your career exploration (college student, early-career professional unsure of direction), dealing with career-related psychological concerns (severe anxiety, depression affecting your job search, identity struggles), or if you need vocational testing to identify strengths and career fit. Career coach: if you know your target direction and need to execute better — improving your resume, landing more interviews, negotiating offers, or accelerating a transition you have already decided to make. Most mid-to-senior professionals who are 'stuck' need a coach, not a counselor."
  - q: "Do career coaches need to be certified?"
    a: "No. Career coaching is an unregulated field — anyone can call themselves a career coach. Common certifications include ICF (International Coaching Federation) credentials (ACC, PCC, MCC), CPCC (Certified Professional Co-Active Coach), and field-specific certifications. The most relevant credential for a job search coach is not certification — it is direct experience in your specific industry, at your specific level, on the hiring side. A former hiring manager at your target company level will often provide more value than a certified coach with no industry experience."
  - q: "How much does a career coach cost vs. a career counselor?"
    a: "Career counselors at university career centers are often free to students and alumni. Private career counselors typically charge $100–$200 per session. Career coaches range from $100–$500 per session for independent coaches, with comprehensive packages (including resume, LinkedIn, interview coaching, and negotiation) ranging from $1,500–$5,000+ depending on level and scope. Executive coaches for C-suite and VP transitions range from $5,000–$20,000+ for full engagements."
  - q: "Can a career coach also provide mental health support?"
    a: "No — and they should not try. Career coaches are not licensed mental health professionals. A good career coach will recognize when a client is experiencing mental health challenges that are beyond the scope of coaching and will recommend therapy or counseling as an appropriate parallel support. The two are complementary, not substitutes. A client dealing with severe job search anxiety, grief from a layoff, or identity crisis from career change often benefits from both a therapist for the emotional processing and a coach for the execution strategy."
